00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:05.570
Chris: Unit testing is an essential instrument
in the toolbox of any serious software developer.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:12.069
A unit test is simply ‘a method that tests a
small portion of an application and verifies

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:14.889
its behaviour independently from other parts’.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.860
Leah: A typical unit test contains 3 phases:

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:24.639
• First, it initializes a small piece of an
application it wants to test which is also

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:28.050
known as the ‘system under test’, or SUT.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:33.709
• Then it applies some stimulus to the system
under test, usually by ‘calling a method on it’,

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.100
• and finally, it observes the resulting behaviour.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:43.430
If the ‘observed behaviour’ is consistent
with the expectations, the unit test passes,

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:49.569
otherwise, it fails, indicating that there is
a problem somewhere within the unit under test.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:55.610
Chris: These three ‘unit test phases’ are
also known as Arrange, Act and Assert, or AAA.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.000
Let’s take a more detailed look at these.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:02.650
Arrange, the first step of a
unit test application, is where

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:04.480
the test is arranged or set up.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:09.560
For example, to perform the test we may need
to create an object of the targeted class,

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:15.039
if necessary, then we need to create mock
objects setting up variables or anything

00:01:15.039 --> 00:01:17.300
else that may be required for the test.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:24.030
Leah: Act, or the middle step of a unit step
application is where we will execute the test and

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:26.999
do the actual unit testing, obtaining a result.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.260
We will call the targeted function
in this step, using the object that

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.860
we created in the previous step.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.730
Assert, the last step of a unit test
application is where we check and verify the

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.479
returned ‘result’ with ‘expected results’

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.239
Chris: Some people also distinguish
a fourth section, ‘teardown’, which

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.829
comes after ‘Arrange, Act, and Assert’.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:53.619
For example, you can use this section
to remove any files created by the test,

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:56.240
close a database connection, and so on.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:00.790
The ‘teardown’ is usually represented
by a separate method, which is

00:02:00.799 --> 00:02:03.360
reused across all tests in the class.

00:02:04.509 --> 00:02:07.550
Leah: Performing unit tests can
have many advantages such as:

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:12.780
Making it safer and easier to refactor
the code by putting tests into a place

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:16.489
that make sure refactoring occurs
without problems and disruption.

00:02:17.099 --> 00:02:19.840
It takes the risk out of
changing older source code.

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:24.820
Carrying out unit tests is essentially
doing quality assurance of the code.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.050
It shows problems and bugs before
the product has an integration test.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:34.619
Creating a testing process before the
coding is completed solves issues and

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.890
challenges creators, to write better code.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.290
It can help find problems and resolve
them before further testing, so they

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:44.070
won’t impact other bits of code.

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:50.650
This includes bugs in a programmer’s execution and
issues with a specification for the unit itself.

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:54.350
Chris: Unit testing maps a
system and creates documentation.

00:02:54.889 --> 00:02:57.500
It helps developers understand
the unit’s interface.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:03.810
Using a unit test and good unit testing tools
means you reduce the overall cost of a project.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.899
Early bug detection means fewer late
changes and easier to spot issues

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.420
than if it is done at a later stage.

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:15.529
It can also help to make you
more efficient at writing code.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:21.670
When you build, run, and fix your code, you
can more quickly find and fix problems because

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:24.000
you are testing your code as you write it.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.610
But, what about the disadvantages?

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:30.330
Leah: Unit testing is a time-consuming process.

00:03:30.599 --> 00:03:36.049
It can take a lot of time to develop even one
small unit test, not to mention the larger ones.

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:42.480
Developers need to spend time on experimentation,
research, figuring out how the test method

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:47.790
should be structured, and what methods,
parameters, and objects, the test case needs.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:51.930
It’s easy to spend an entire
day on one single ‘unit test’.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:57.109
Not only is time spent creating the ‘unit
test’ itself, but also it takes time

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.960
to figure out the best way to use it.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:04.960
Unit tests are problematic when you need
to test your ‘user interface’ or UI.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:10.849
They are good for when you need to test business
logic implementation, but not great for UI.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:15.850
Chris: Unit testing for individual components
of a software system, will catch many

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:17.959
errors, but they can’t catch them all.

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:24.070
For example, it can’t catch ‘integration bugs’
which occur when combining several units of work.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:30.229
To catch an ‘integration bug’, an integration test
is needed which we cover elsewhere in this unit.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:35.740
So even though there are disadvantages,
we can see that unit testing is valuable

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:40.019
tool to detect defects in the software,
in the early development stage.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.480
No matter what code you are testing, finding
bugs and errors ahead of time is helpful.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:51.800
If you are working with a good QA team, using
unit tests should be an efficient process

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.200
and prove helpful, if you know what to avoid.

